3. The TODO comment states that wikitext data should not blindly write to disk without any validation or sanitisation which can cause a potential issue if the wikitext is set to an arbitrary user-supplied value (e.g., through request parameter).
4. Also, there's no rate limiting on HTTP requests so it might be susceptible to DDoS attacks. 
5. It seems that FaviconData and CssData are hardcoded which might not be secure if these contain sensitive data like passwords or API keys.
6. The code snippet doesn't seem to have any input validation on http_request_get_path_info(req) and http_request_get_query_string(req), so it can cause potential problems with malicious requests if user-supplied data is directly passed into these functions without proper validation.
</analysis>

