<analysis>
The get_output function uses the check_output method from Python's subprocess module to execute shell commands and returns the stdout output as bytes. The potential security issue here is a Shell Injection vulnerability. If the command input contains user-supplied data or untrusted inputs, it can be exploited by an attacker.
For example, if the function expects users to input a file name like "/etc/passwd", but an attacker could pass in "rm -rf /;touch /tmp/blah", this will delete all files from the root directory and create a new file named "/tmp/blah". 
This issue is rated as High severity according to CWE-78 (Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command) because it can be exploited for arbitrary code execution.
</analysis>

