<analysis>
The provided code snippet appears to be a part of a system test using Python with pytest and kubectl commands. The line containing the nslookup command doesn't seem to have any obvious security vulnerabilities, but there are few considerations we need to keep in mind. 

1) Kubernetes Command Execution: The script is running a command `kubectl --kubeconfig=/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf exec -ti {0} nslookup {1}` using the sudo privileges of the host machine. If this pod has elevated permissions, it could escalate those permissive actions or even gain unauthorised access to system resources.

2) Input Validation: The provided variables (pod_name and hostname) are directly used in a format string without any validation. These might contain unexpected inputs which can cause unwanted behaviors. 

3) No Error Handling: The script only checks the exit code of the command, but doesn't parse or handle possible stderr output to get more detailed information about potential problems.

CWE-89 (Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')) - Low Confidence. Kubernetes and Pod names may contain some special characters which could be exploited if not properly validated, but this depends on the overall system design and security measures applied by other parts of your application.

CWE-93 (Reliance on Untrusted Randomness) - Medium Confidence. The code doesn't seem to have any dependencies on randomness that could be exploited for unpredictable results, but this again is dependent on the overall system design.
</analysis>

