<analysis>
The provided `_exec_cmd` function contains a potential security issue related to shell command injection. The argument passed into this method is directly used in the subprocess call without any sanitisation or parameterisation, which could potentially lead to an attacker injecting malicious commands that are then executed with higher privileges than intended by the original program.

The code doesn't seem to be vulnerable to other common attacks like cross-site scripting (XSS), insecure direct object references (IDOR) etc as it is not dealing directly with user inputs or manipulating any kind of data structure from a database which could potentially lead to sensitive information disclosure. 

However, the function uses shell=True which means that it can take advantage of shell features like variable expansion and wildcard characters. If there are arguments containing special characters that get interpreted by the underlying shell, this might be exploitable in certain circumstances.

In summary, while this specific code block does not seem to have a serious security vulnerability, its use as part of a larger system could potentially lead to other issues when used together with untrusted input. Therefore, it is recommended that thorough review and testing are conducted on all parts of the application interacting with user inputs before deployment.
</analysis>

